# Purpose
Keep TS strict and trustworthy to reduce runtime bugs and enable safe refactors.

## Do / Don't

### DO
DO assume strict TypeScript (including strictNullChecks) and code defensively.
DO type module boundaries explicitly:
  - API client functions
  - storage adapters
  - reusable hooks
  - navigation/route-param helpers
DO prefer inference for locals; add annotations where they define contracts.
DO use unknown for untrusted data and narrow via type guards.
DO model errors as typed unions (e.g., { kind: 'network' | 'http' | 'parse' ... }).

### DON'T
MUST NOT use any unless unavoidable:
  - If used, add // any: <reason> and immediately narrow at the boundary.
MUST NOT use blind casts (as Foo) to silence errors; validate/narrow instead.
MUST NOT use non-null assertions (!) except for proven invariants (and explain why).

## Completion Checklist
[ ] No new any without an inline justification comment.
[ ] Untrusted inputs are unknown and narrowed safely.
[ ] Public functions/hooks have explicit input/output types.
[ ] No unsafe param parsing without guards.

## Common Pitfalls
Treating API responses as trusted objects.
NaN/undefined bugs from unguarded conversions (Number(x) without checks).
Over-annotating everything and fighting inference.
